WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.086
(tribal music)

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.304
- Raya and the Last Dragon

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:16.850
tells a story of Raya who is a lone warrior

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.561
living in the world of Kumandra.

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:22.647
After being separated from her family

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:25.483
living in a divided world, she takes it

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:28.778
upon herself to unify
all the disparate lands

00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:33.783
of Kumandra and bring
the world back together.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:34.784
Hello everyone.

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:37.454
My name is Carlos López Estrada,

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:41.583
I am one of the two directors
of Raya and the Last Dragon.

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:44.127
- Hi everyone, my name Fawn Veerasunthorn.

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:46.463
I'm the Head of Story on
Raya and the Last Dragon.

00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:48.006
The scene you're about to see

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:51.426
after the whole action
packed scene earlier,

00:00:51.426 --> 00:00:53.803
we just wanted to single
in on Raya's relationship

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:54.929
with her dad.

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:57.891
- [Carlos] The purpose of
the scene is to introduce us

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.559
to the world of Kumandra,

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:03.313
to show us that there's
five different lands

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:07.233
and that they are
perceived as enemy lands.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:11.237
And then Benja very cleverly uses the soup

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:14.324
as a tool to explain
to her that even though

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.951
the lands are separate, they shouldn't be.

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:21.247
- [Fawn] In the first shot
we use a lot of the technique

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.208
of the frame within frame for this film.

00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:25.627
And that was inspired
from our research trip

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:27.629
where we go through Southeast Asia.

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:28.463
A lot of the places we've been

00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:31.591
there's a lot of the use of long corridors,

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.384
and anywhere you look or take photos

00:01:33.384 --> 00:01:35.178
there's a frame within the frame

00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:37.889
and the top of each of
these building structures

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:40.934
you see the principle of
using repetition in design,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.645
and that is the very
principle of Southeast Asian

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:46.147
temple design that we kind of adapt.

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:48.983
And in the next shot,
you'll see a little bit

00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:50.944
of the fantasy animal to make sure

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:55.782
that you clock that this
is not a real world.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:58.201
And in this shot we want
to show the daily life

00:01:58.201 --> 00:01:59.619
in Southeast Asia.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.205
And every morning you
go and you do something

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:04.332
to pay respects to the invisible world.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.920
- We decided to add this like cloth wipe

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.298
transition just to make
it a little bit snappier.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:16.344
I think we probably saw like
10 versions of this animation

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:18.388
just 'cause we like to explore things

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:19.889
until we're out of time.

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:23.309
And then we landed on
a very simple solution,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.435
but it felt it right.

00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:25.937
You know what I was just thinking Fawn?

00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:28.731
If we could go back and do over,

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:31.734
I would probably add like a
little bit of a focus shift.

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:33.695
You know, the background could
have been a little blurry

00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:36.156
and then we do like a
shift to define them.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:38.825
Next time.

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:42.328
I think we specifically liked here the,

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:45.331
the contrast between Benja and Raya,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.917
you see him like walking all proper

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.420
hands behind his back
and then Raya comes in

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.839
just like kicking and
screaming and rolling around.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:56.342
And it just very clearly
shows the two characters.

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:57.969
I would like to point out that Fawn

00:02:57.969 --> 00:03:01.181
was one of the key
promoters of making Benja

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:03.057
look so attractive.

00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:04.058
- And Adele Lim.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:05.393
Can't leave her out of that.

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:08.646
- Adele Lim, the New York
Times wrote a whole article

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.858
featuring Benja, Benja's hotness.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.651
So thank you Fawn and Adele.

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:16.613
- I'm glad you feel prepared dew drop,

00:03:16.613 --> 00:03:19.032
because I have something
important to tell you.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:22.285
- If you ever zoom into
their fabric pattern.

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:24.329
What we found is that in Southeast Asia

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:26.873
if there's specific flowers or animals

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:29.083
that's really common in that area,

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:30.919
it will show up in what they wear.

00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:35.506
Heart is about water and the
relationship with the dragon.

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:37.050
And if you look at Raya's cape,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.510
at the bottom there's
an image of rain drop.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.846
- Even if you look here at the handrail

00:03:41.846 --> 00:03:44.515
and the figure that is
repeated throughout,

00:03:44.515 --> 00:03:47.560
it's a water drop and
there's a lot of that motif

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.728
throughout all of Heart.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.855
- Tell me what you know
about the other lands.

00:03:50.855 --> 00:03:52.065
- First, Tail.

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:53.733
- [Carlos] For this little sequence,

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.777
everything has outlines.

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:57.904
The colors are rendered a lot flatter.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.198
It gave us this really interesting look

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:03.409
that we liked just whenever
Raya went into her fantasies.

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.078
And you can see this
where we're talking about

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:06.412
like the land identities.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.040
If you look at the first one this is Tail,

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.542
it's at the very, very edge of Kumandra.

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:13.503
It's an arid landscape.

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:17.590
They live off of the land
and their colors are warm

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.133
and yellow and earthy toned.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.427
So you get to see it very clearly here.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.722
Fawn, anything that you
want to say about Talon?

00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:25.723
- I just wanna point out how,

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:28.268
if you ever walk the night
market anywhere in Asia,

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:30.228
this is the exact vibe you'll get.

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:32.272
And I thought it would
be awesome to put durian

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:35.024
in here even though to
throw durian in the air

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:37.735
and cut it like this, it's
probably not that realistic,

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:41.698
but hey, I don't know when
else I will see durian

00:04:41.698 --> 00:04:43.324
in a Disney movie.

00:04:43.324 --> 00:04:46.828
- Durian is this incredible
fruit from the region

00:04:46.828 --> 00:04:49.372
that is specific for many reasons,

00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:53.126
but one of them is the
incredibly strong smell.

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.669
- Yeah, and we thought that it's important

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:56.713
that Dang Hai is not like, "Ugh it smells."

00:04:56.713 --> 00:05:00.300
He's like, "Yes, smells good."

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.134
- Spine.

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:02.719
- So, this is land of Spine.

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:06.389
Spine is up north in the mountains.

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:10.310
Spine has the fame of being
this home of warrior community

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.396
and they're formidable and
they have like a squinty face.

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:15.606
Like I'm gonna destroy you kind of look,

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:19.152
the last of our lands is Fang.

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.946
As you can tell all the geometry is sharp,

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:24.574
they got insane looking battle cats.

00:05:24.574 --> 00:05:28.119
So we here introduce them
as like the ultimate baddy.

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:31.122
And that shows you really sort
of like one of the main ideas

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:34.167
in the movie, which is that
we really shouldn't be making

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.627
assumptions of people
based on where they live.

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:40.256
Also, there's a hidden Mickey
in this little Fang bit.

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:44.677
So, if you want to pause
it somewhere in there,

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:48.097
you may or may not find the silhouette

00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:50.767
of Mickey Mouse hidden somewhere in here.

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:51.934
I'm not gonna tell you where it is,

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.644
'cause then that defeats the purpose.

00:05:53.644 --> 00:05:56.272
- Okay, so we're gonna
need some cross bows,

00:05:56.272 --> 00:06:00.860
and catapults and oh, what
about flaming catapults?

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.070
- [Fawn] We always had this passion

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.574
to put food in this film and
I took a Thai cooking class.

00:06:06.574 --> 00:06:08.701
And I remember the chef
at the time was saying

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.495
that like Southeast Asian cooking

00:06:10.495 --> 00:06:13.331
is just about putting things that on paper

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:14.457
they don't go well together.

00:06:14.457 --> 00:06:18.086
Like bitter, salty, sweet, you
know, all those five flavors.

00:06:18.086 --> 00:06:19.379
But somehow when they come together

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:22.715
that is reflective of
this region's lifestyle,

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.717
like people coming from different places.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:28.012
And it's such a perfect way to
state the theme of the movie.

00:06:28.012 --> 00:06:32.392
- I was trying to convince Fawn
that we should eat some soup

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:34.977
while we did this interview today.

00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:39.315
And she kindly passed on the idea.

00:06:40.942 --> 00:06:44.028
- We were once unified,
harmoniously as one.

00:06:44.028 --> 00:06:45.363
- We were thinking of this scene,

00:06:45.363 --> 00:06:48.157
sort of as our Simba and Mufasa moment

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:49.992
at the top of the Lion King,

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:53.913
where Mufasa sort of like states
the big idea of the movie.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:56.999
And even though he's not
around for the rest of it

00:06:56.999 --> 00:07:00.378
like the philosophy resonates
so strongly in Simba

00:07:00.378 --> 00:07:02.672
that it's essentially what drives him.

00:07:02.672 --> 00:07:06.551
And this scene played like
a very similar function.

00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:08.970
- [Fawn] When I see this
shot, I'm reminded of like

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.974
anytime that we talk
about Kumandra and harmony

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:16.060
and togetherness, like the
use of the bokeh effects.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:18.312
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:18.312 --> 00:07:21.607
Long lenses with like
beautiful blurry background.

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:23.192
And you see all the bokeh lights,

00:07:23.192 --> 00:07:26.404
just like really, really
dreamy and aspirational.

00:07:26.404 --> 00:07:27.655
So to wrap things up,

00:07:27.655 --> 00:07:29.824
I would say that it's
also a good opportunity

00:07:29.824 --> 00:07:31.826
to talk about our our set extension

00:07:31.826 --> 00:07:33.494
and matte painting, which is you know,

00:07:33.494 --> 00:07:35.913
very nerdy animation terms.

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:38.958
But so a set extension is just everything

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.878
that goes or like beyond what's built.

00:07:41.878 --> 00:07:45.256
In this particular shot,
Raya and Benja are obviously

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:47.967
CG characters that are
fully three dimensional.

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:51.721
The building that they're in is also CG

00:07:51.721 --> 00:07:53.681
and fully three dimensional.

00:07:53.681 --> 00:07:55.892
And then everything beyond that,

00:07:55.892 --> 00:07:59.228
like the mountain range,
the river, the sky,

00:07:59.228 --> 00:08:02.690
is painted flatly and these
are called matte paintings.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:05.109
And then it's all composited together.

00:08:05.109 --> 00:08:07.403
I've only been around Disney
Animation for a little bit,

00:08:07.403 --> 00:08:11.199
but they tell us that this
scene utilizes set extension

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:13.743
and matte painting
techniques that have never

00:08:13.743 --> 00:08:16.287
been done before, at
least in Disney Animation.

00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:19.373
Just because the level of detail
and the scope of our world

00:08:19.373 --> 00:08:24.378
was so, so huge that there's
so many matte paintings.

00:08:24.921 --> 00:08:26.839
And as a button, I will just say,

00:08:27.715 --> 00:08:30.843
just imagine how much
cooler it would've been

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:32.136
if we had been eating soup

00:08:32.136 --> 00:08:34.347
throughout this whole thing, Fawn.

00:08:37.225 --> 00:08:38.142
Next time.

00:08:38.142 --> 00:08:40.186
- You're right, Carlos.

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:42.563
Next time we'll be eating on camera.

